.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt ATTO 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm atto
.Nd simple modeless text editor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm atto
.Op Fl C
.Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a screen-based text editor.
It serves as an alternative to
.Xr vi 1
for users who are not accustomed to text editors with separate insertion
and editing modes, and includes support for editing UTF-8 text.
.Pp
Perhaps all that needs to be known to use
.Nm
at a basic level is that holding the control key, pressing x,
then pressing w will write the currently open file, and holding the
control key, pressing x, then pressing c will exit the editor.
.Pp
For users who struggle to remember key bindings, the escape key can
be pressed twice to open a menu which can be navigated with the
terminal's cursor keys.
This is only available if the program is not compiled in
.Dv SMALL
mode (i.e. it is not part of a ramdisk or similar).
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -compact -width Fl
.It Fl C
Use colours, if available, for basic language-unaware syntax highlighting.
.El
.Ss Cursor movement commands
C is used as an abbreviation for the control key, commonly rendered as
"ctrl" on English language keyboards.
.Pp
Arrow keys or the navigation cluster can also be used to move the
cursor when the keyboard has such features.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "xxspacexx" -compact -offset indent
.It C-a
move cursor to beginning of current line (i.e. "home")
.It C-b
move cursor back one character (i.e. "left")
.It C-e
move cursor to end of current line (i.e. "end")
.It C-f
move cursor forward one character (i.e. "right")
.It C-n
move cursor to next line (i.e. "down")
.It C-p
move cursor to previous line (i.e. "up")
.It C-v
move cursor to next page of text (i.e. "page down")
.It Esc-b
move cursor back one word
.It Esc-f
move cursor forward one word
.It Esc-g
jump to a specific line number in the file
.It Esc-v
move cursor to previous page of text (i.e. "page up")
.It Esc-<
jump to the start of the file
.It Esc->
jump to the end of the file
.El
.Ss Text editing commands
.Bl -tag -width "xxspacexx" -compact -offset indent
.It C-d
delete character under cursor (i.e. "del")
.It C-h
move cursor backwards and delete previous character (i.e. "backspace")
.It C-g
abort at prompts (cancel task when asked a question)
.It C-i
insert tab character
.It C-j
insert newline character (i.e. "enter", "return")
.It C-k
cut text up to the end of the line (i.e. "kill line")
.It C-l
refresh the screen
.It C-m
insert carriage return character
.It C-r
search for text backwards, jump to next result
.It C-s
search for text forwards, jump to next result
.It C-y
paste the text in the cut buffer (i.e. "yank")
.It Esc-r
search for text and replace it
.El
.Ss Buffer commands
.Nm
supports multiple editing buffers, allowing users to edit multiple
files from a single instance of the program.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "xxspacexx" -compact -offset indent
.It C-x C-c
close all open buffers and exit
.Nm ,
prompting if changes would be discarded
.It C-x C-f
open a file into a new buffer
.It C-x C-k
close the current buffer, prompting if changes would be
discarded (i.e. "kill")
.It C-x C-s
save current buffer to disk, overwriting the file
.It C-x C-w
save current buffer to disk, prompting for file name ("write")
.It C-x n
cycle to next buffer
.It C-x i
read another file and insert it into the current buffer
.It C-x =
display cursor position in current buffer
.El
.Ss Region commands
As well as cutting text on a line basis (i.e. C-k), a region
can be selected by setting a mark at the beginning of the
region, and then moving the cursor to the end of the region.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "xxspacexx" -compact -offset indent
.It C-space
set the beginning of the region at the current position
.It C-w
cut the marked region
.It Esc-w
copy the marked region without deleting it
.El
.Ss Window management commands
Simple split-screen window management is included.
For more advanced use cases, consider using
.Xr tmux 1 .
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "xxspacexx" -compact -offset indent
.It C-x 1
delete windows except the currently active one
.It C-x 2
split current window into two windows
.It C-x o
switch to the next open window
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ed 1 ,
.Xr ex 1 ,
.Xr vi 1 ,
.Xr xedit 1
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
is not the standard editor.
.Sh AUTHORS
.Nm
was written primarily by
.An Hugh Barney ,
and was originally based on public domain code from Anthony Howe's
editor (commonly known as Anthony's Editor).
.Pp
It has been modified for inclusion in
.Nx ,
including with enhanced option handling and menu support.
